WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:04.567
A new programme needs an HSSE function. An
operating asset needs

2
00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:09.233
stronger controls. A board needs evidence it
can act on. Ascendera’s

3
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:13.333
practice offers three ways to engage: build
it, run it, or

4
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:13.833
prove it.

5
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:19.500
When a programme starts, we establish its
policy, governance, organisation,

6
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:24.667
competence requirements and risk-based
budget. We develop or amend the

7
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:29.367
management system in your templates. You
receive a workable structure, standards

8
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.933
and a first-year plan with clear owners and
dates.

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.633
Where you need us to run the function, we
provide accountable

10
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:40.133
leadership and specialist practitioners
under an agreed managed-service scope.

11
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:45.533
Your board sets the direction. We manage the
programme, contractor controls,

12
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:49.700
assurance and reporting, with a handover
plan from day one.

13
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.100
We focus on the risks that can cause the
greatest harm:

14
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:59.033
work at height, lifting operations, plant
and pedestrian interfaces, confined

15
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:05.100
spaces, excavation, and hazardous energy.
Energy isolation and control of stored

16
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.800
or residual energy are a core part of that
work, including

17
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.667
electrical charge, hydraulic and pneumatic
pressure, mechanical tension and

18
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:18.400
raised equipment. Our practitioners define
the critical controls and verify that

19
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.133
they are present and effective. You receive
a critical risk register

20
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:24.433
and a verification regime matched to the
work.

21
00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:28.933
Switching off a supply does not remove every
hazard. Stored pressure,

22
00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:34.167
electrical charge, mechanical tension,
gravity and residual heat may remain.

23
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:39.367
Effective control identifies every energy
source, isolates and secures it,

24
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.067
releases or restrains stored energy, and
verifies the safe state before

25
00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:49.900
work begins. Where energy can reaccumulate,
verification continues. The controls

26
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.367
follow an equipment-specific isolation
procedure.

27
00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:57.167
Critical-risk scope also covers dropped
objects and line-of-fire exposure,

28
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:03.333
electrical work, pressure testing, hot work,
fire and explosion, and the

29
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:07.500
stability of temporary works. The controls
and verification are matched to

30
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.633
the actual activity.

31
00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:14.267
For process safety, we facilitate hazard
identification and HAZOP studies,

32
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.133
connect hazards to barriers, and define
safety-critical elements and performance

33
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:24.300
standards. We support human-factors reviews
and commissioning assurance. The

34
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.833
result is a barrier register with owners,
requirements and verification dates.

35
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.567
Before award, we assess contractor
capability against the actual scope and

36
00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:38.067
agreed criteria. Before mobilisation, we
check readiness for the work about

37
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:42.433
to begin. You receive an assessment record,
a readiness evidence pack

38
00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:45.133
and clear actions to address gaps before
exposure.

39
00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:50.400
For boards, owners and lenders, we provide
independent assurance against an

40
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.400
agreed requirement set. Field evidence
records where, when and who verified

41
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.367
the control. Confidence reflects the
evidence available. Independent

42
00:03:01.367 --> 00:03:05.033
verification is kept separate from delivery
of the controls being assessed.

43
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.633
When an assessment identifies a gap, we
write the proposed fix

44
00:03:09.633 --> 00:03:14.267
in your own documents and templates. We
assign action owners, support

45
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:19.833
supervisor and verifier training, and return
to test closure. Maturity reviews,

46
00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:24.500
repeated assessments and incident learning
show whether changes have reached the

47
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:24.900
work.

48
00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.833
The practice also supports emergency
preparedness and crisis exercises,

49
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:36.233
occupational health, fatigue and wellbeing.
Environmental controls, worker

50
00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:40.133
welfare and social performance can form part
of the agreed scope.

51
00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:44.200
The focus is on the obligations and
conditions of your project.

52
00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:49.567
Our digital tools read the records, connect
findings and maintain registers.

53
00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:54.700
Practitioners interpret the evidence and
make the professional judgements. This

54
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.233
puts more attention at the workface and
gives leadership a clearer

55
00:03:58.233 --> 00:04:02.600
view across the estate. Your information and
resulting registers remain yours.

56
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:07.533
Start with one asset, one critical risk, a
system that needs

57
00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:13.400
fixing, or the whole HSSE function. We agree
the requirement, examine

58
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.500
the evidence and define what you will
receive. Build it. Run

59
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.033
it. Prove it. Ascendera.
